      "writtenOn": "2023-02-21T13:16:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The distinction between `smccc` and `smccc::trng` is that the SMCCC TRNG ABI (in particular, return values) doesn\u0027t follow the SMCCC ABI so they need different `Error` types and different `Result`, which in turn means that they need to use different helper functions for the `HVC`.\n\nThe `super::hvc64` call isn\u0027t `smccc::trng` depending on `smccc` but both using the low-level asm wrapper that takes and returns arrays of register values and is expected to be provided by an external crate but is, for now, defined in `smccc`.\n\nSo the hierarchy of wrappers is:\n\n1. wrap the `asm!` in `smccc::hvc64([u64; _]) -\u003e [u64; _]`\n2. wrap the standard return type from `smccc::hvc64` in `smccc::checked_hvc64([u64; _]) -\u003e smccc::Result` and `smccc::trng::hvc64([u64; _]) -\u003e smccc::trng::Result`\n3. wrap the individual functions in `hvc::{mem,mmio_guard,trng}_X` to encapsulate their FID and make Rust aware of their respective API (number of arguments and return value)\n\nI added the high-level functions to `hvc::` to be consistent with what we already had there but agree that they could also have been placed in `smccc::trng`. WDYT?",
